0.2 (unreleased) — Qt 6 port in progress
Build system:
    Add a QtWebEngineWidgets smoke test (autotests/smoke) that verifies the Qt 6.11.3 toolchain can run WebEngine headless before porting begins.
    Port the qmake build to Qt 6: QT += webenginewidgets webchannel printsupport uitools core5compat replaces webkit; webkittrunk.pri and the Qt4/OS2 conditionals are gone. Only a skeleton builds for now — main.cpp launches a stub window with a QWebEngineView while the rest of src/ waits on the per-module port tasks.

Core web layer (MIG02):
    Port WebView/WebPage/WebActionMapper to QWebEngineView/QWebEnginePage. Context menus use QWebEngineContextMenuRequest, downloads go through QWebEngineDownloadRequest (saved to the default download directory until the download manager is ported), JavaScript window objects ("external", "arora") are exposed via QWebChannel, and failed loads show the Arora notfound.html error page again. Custom user agent strings apply through QWebEngineProfile::httpUserAgent.
    The Ctrl access-key link hints are dropped: they needed synchronous DOM access that WebEngine does not provide.
    "Open in New Tab" context menu entries open a standalone window until the tab widget is ported; bookmark-link, "Search with..." and add-to-toolbar-search entries are temporarily disabled while the bookmarks and opensearch modules are still being ported.

Cookie layer (MIG03):
    Port the cookie jar and its dialogs to QWebEngineCookieStore on the application QWebEngineProfile. Cookies are now persisted by the WebEngine profile itself; the accept/keep policy and the domain exception lists (block / allow / allow-for-session) are enforced through the store's cookie filter and stored in the application settings (cookies.ini is gone). "Accept only from sites navigated to" now blocks third-party cookies.
    Private browsing selects an off-the-record WebEngine profile instead of a global flag — pages already open keep their profile, newly created windows/tabs are private.

Network layer (MIG04):
    The application keeps a slimmed-down QNetworkAccessManager (proxy settings, disk cache, HTTP auth/proxy-auth/SSL-error dialogs, Accept-Language) for its own fetches — OpenSearch downloads and adblock subscriptions. Web page traffic no longer passes through it: Chromium owns web networking, the adblock hook moves to a profile request interceptor, and the form-post autofill hook is gone with WebKit.
    file:// directory listings are served by a custom "arora-file" URL scheme (QWebEngineUrlSchemeHandler on the profile) — navigating to a local directory redirects to it and renders the classic Arora listing with icons.
    The QWebPage-tagging proxy classes (WebPageProxy, NetworkAccessManagerProxy) are deleted; WebPage now derives straight from QWebEnginePage.

History (MIG06):
    Port the history store, models, dialog, menu and location-bar completer to Qt 6. QtWebKit's QWebHistoryInterface is gone — WebEngine keeps Chromium's own internal history — so WebPage now feeds the application store from its loadFinished/titleChanged/iconChanged signals. Pages on an off-the-record profile never reach it; that is the private-browsing guarantee now.
    Normal browsing now runs on a named, persistent WebEngine profile ("arora"): QWebEngineProfile::defaultProfile() is itself off-the-record, so the previous wiring left cookies, cache and storage non-persistent.
    Favicons in the history views come from a memory-only icon cache fed by QWebEnginePage::iconChanged — WebEngine has no application-visible on-disk icon database.

Downloads (MIG05):
    The download manager now drives QWebEngineDownloadRequest: Chromium streams files to disk itself once the item has picked a name and accepted the request. The download list UI, progress/speed/ETA display, "Try Again", open-with-desktop and the external download program option are kept. Suggested server file names are reduced to a bare file name and de-duplicated with a -N suffix so existing files are never silently overwritten.
    "Try Again" re-issues the download through its page (or a hidden page when the original is gone, e.g. items restored from disk) and re-attaches the fresh request to the same row. The default download directory moved from the desktop to the platform Downloads folder.

Bookmarks (MIG07):
    Port the bookmarks store, model, dialogs, menu and toolbar to Qt 6. The bookmarks file moved from QDesktopServices' data location to the standard application-data directory (still bookmarks.xbel). Bookmark favicons come from the history manager's icon cache — WebEngine has no application icon database.
    The XBEL reader no longer supports arbitrary undeclared entities (Qt 6 removed QXmlStreamEntityResolver); "&nbsp;" is still expanded. Until the application object lands, opening bookmarks via Ctrl/middle-click modifiers behaves like a plain click.

Location bar + search (MIG08):
    Port the location bar (site icon, privacy indicator, load progress), the search banner widget and the OpenSearch stack to Qt 6 / WebEngine. Search suggestions are parsed with QJsonDocument now that QtScript is gone; engine definitions load/save through the OpenSearch XML reader/writer as before, stored in the standard application-data directory.
    "Add '<engine>'" entries for page-advertised OpenSearch descriptions are collected asynchronously from the render process and drop into the search button's menu as they arrive. The private-browsing indicator follows the page's off-the-record profile; clicking it no longer exits private mode yet — that needs the application object (setPrivate is a whole-window affair under WebEngine).
    The JavaScript window.external.AddSearchProvider hook and the start page's arora.currentEngine/searchUrl bindings are live again via QWebChannel.

AdBlock (MIG09):
    Port the AdBlock stack to Qt WebEngine: requests are now blocked by a QWebEngineUrlRequestInterceptor installed on the browsing profile (Chromium fails them with net::ERR_ACCESS_DENIED), replacing the QNetworkAccessManager hook WebKit used. Rule parsing, subscriptions, the configuration dialog and "abp:subscribe" links are kept — abp: is now a registered custom URL scheme whose handler shows the subscribe prompt.
    Element-hiding ("##") rules are injected as a stylesheet into loaded pages instead of walking the DOM; as a side benefit they also hide matching elements added after the page loads. The "Block Image" context menu entry works again, feeding a custom rule into the dialog.

AdBlock (ADB01):
    Upgrade the filter engine toward uBlock-Origin coverage: rules now understand resource-type options, third-party/first-party and domain=/denyallow= restrictions, $important, $badfilter, $match-case and the document/elemhide/generichide/genericblock page exceptions. Matched requests can be redirected to bundled stub resources via $redirect=/$redirect-rule= (served from the new arora-resource:// scheme — transparent pixels, noop.js, empty VAST/VMAP and friends), and $removeparam strips tracking query parameters instead of blocking.
    Cosmetic filtering understands the extended syntax: :-abp-has/-abp-contains style selectors, :has-text, :matches-css, :style, :remove*, :upward and a small +js scriptlet subset (set-constant, abort-on-property-read/write, hide-if-contains, remove-class), including #@#/#?#/#$# exception and snippet markers.
    Default subscriptions now point at live lists — EasyList and EasyPrivacy from easylist.to plus the uBlock "filters" list — and stored subscriptions on the dead adblockplus.mozdev.org/easylist.adblockplus.org hosts are migrated automatically, keeping their list file names.

AdBlock (ADB02):
    Optional alternate filter engine: configuring with `qmake CONFIG+=adblock_rust` (after `cargo build --release` in src/adblock/rust, needing a user-local Rust toolchain) delegates request matching to Brave's adblock-rust engine over a small C FFI wrapper — subscriptions, the dialog and the UI are unchanged. The engine applies native uBO semantics for $badfilter, $denyallow, $important, $removeparam, $redirect= to bundled stubs and cosmetic selectors; the built-in C++ matcher stays the default.
    `./arora --adblock-rust-smoke` diffs both engines on a fixed rule+URL probe set.

AdBlock (ADB03):
    The ad-block dialog's action menu gained "Preset Filter Lists...": a curated catalog of community lists grouped by Core & Privacy, Annoyances, Security, Resource Abuse & Mining and Regional (uBlock Origin's uAssets lists, EasyList Annoyances/Cookie Notices/Social, the maintained NoCoin anti-miner list, phishing/malware URL blocklists, and German/French/Russian/Czech-Slovak/Lithuanian regional coverage). Ticking a preset subscribes through the existing subscription machinery — explicit opt-in, counting as the remote-list download consent — unticking disables the subscription; nothing is subscribed automatically.
    Filter-list parsing now accepts files that open with "!" comment metadata instead of the "[Adblock Plus ...]" banner (uAssets and the malware-filter lists lead this way — previously they were rejected and deleted), and easylist-downloads.adblockplus.org was dropped from the dead-host migration list since the host is live and the rewrite produced broken regional-list URLs.

AdBlock (ADB04):
    The AdBlock Configuration dialog was redesigned around subscriptions instead of a per-rule tree: one checkbox enables or disables a whole list (with its rule count and last-update time shown), backed by Update Now and Remove buttons, while remote lists are never expanded into rows — a downloaded EasyList used to explode into tens of thousands of comment and metadata lines that could not be toggled anyway, making the dialog slow to open and unusable. A checkable "Show rules in the selected list" section offers read-only rule inspection with a search filter for the rare cases it is needed.
    Custom rules are now a plain-text editor — one rule per line, "!" comments allowed — committed to the existing custom-rules subscription shortly after typing stops and always on close, so edits apply live like the old inline rows. The per-rule enable checkbox on remote lists is gone with the tree: to suspend a single remote rule, comment it out or use the per-site whitelist from the shield panel instead.

Autofill (MIG10):
    Password form saving and filling is back, reimplemented for WebEngine: an injected script (src/data/autofill.js, replacing parseForms.js) fills stored credentials after each page load and reports form submits through a per-page QWebChannel object instead of the old QNetworkAccessManager POST hook, which no longer exists. The stored-forms list, the save-password prompt ("Never for this site" / "Not now"), the preferences pane and the Form Passwords dialog are unchanged, and the autofill.dat store keeps its format.
    Private windows (off-the-record profile) still get forms filled but are never recorded. Feature deltas vs the WebKit version: submits inside iframes and submits fired before the page's channel handshake completes can be missed, and a submit that navigates immediately may race the report delivery — WebEngine gives no way to observe the POST itself.

Settings and private data (MIG11):
    Port the preferences dialog, accept-language dialog and Clear Private Data to WebEngine. Settings now live on a shared QWebEngineProfile (fonts, JavaScript/images/popups/plugins toggles, disk cache size, Accept-Language header) applied through a new BrowserProfile helper; the custom user style sheet is injected as a page script since WebEngine has no setUserStyleSheetUrl. Clear Private Data wipes history, favicons, downloads, search-box history, cookies, Chromium visited links and both the WebEngine and application disk caches. Feature deltas vs the WebKit version: the "maximum pages in memory cache" option and the Ctrl access-key hint toggle are gone (no WebEngine equivalent), and Clear Private Data no longer needs to restart the browser to empty the cache.

Plugins (MIG13):
    Remove all Flash/plugin machinery: modern browsers ship no NPAPI plugin support and neither does WebEngine. The src/qwebplugins/ subtree (QWebPluginFactory wrapper, AroraWebPlugin, ClickToFlash with its whitelist dialog) is deleted, along with the "Use ClickToFlash on flash plugins" checkbox, its "enableClickToFlash" settings key and the related translation strings. The generic "Enable Plugins" web-content toggle remains (it maps to QWebEngineSettings::PluginsEnabled).
    Dead utils code deleted with it: EditListView (only used by the ClickToFlash settings dialog, autotest removed too) and the unused Windows-only ExplorerStyle. utils.pri is retired — the remaining utils classes are listed directly in src.pri. SingleApplication (single-instance via QLocalServer/QLocalSocket) is ported to Qt 6 for the upcoming application-entry task.

View source + page search (MIG12):
    Port the source viewer, source syntax highlighter, plain-text search helper and the page find bar to Qt 6 / WebEngine. Page find now drives the asynchronous QWebEnginePage::findText and reports match counts and the active match from QWebEngineFindTextResult; "highlight all occurrences" is emulated by re-running the search since WebEngine has no such flag.
    View Source re-fetches the page through the application network access manager and shows the raw wire bytes when they match the DOM serialization (a probe WebEngine page performs the comparison asynchronously), otherwise the live DOM dump; the syntax highlighter now runs on QRegularExpression.

Browser chrome (MIG14):
    Port the assembled browser window, tab widget, tab bar and the user-agent menu to Qt 6 / WebEngine. Menus, toolbars and shortcuts drive the page through QWebEnginePage::WebAction; history navigation uses QWebEngineHistory; printing goes through QWebEngineView::print(QPrinter*); View Source uses the asynchronous page::toHtml.
    The application class (BrowserApplication) is now compiled in and supplies the profile-aware new window/tab plumbing: target=_blank links and Ctrl/Shift-modified link clicks open background or foreground tabs through WebPage::createWindow, and private-browsing windows run on the off-the-record WebEngine profile.
    Feature deltas vs the WebKit version: "zoom text only" is kept as a preference but WebEngine always zooms the whole page; the WebKit page inspector shortcut and global icon database are gone; the text-encoding menu still applies per page via QWebEngineSettings; session save/restore serializes each tab's WebEngine history items explicitly since they have no stream operators.

App entry (MIG15):
    `arora` runs as the real application again instead of the migration skeleton: main() constructs BrowserApplication, single-instance forwarding is back (a second `arora <url>` hands the url to the running instance and exits), session save/restore works, and the startup-behavior preference (homepage / blank page / restore session / url operand) is honored. Command-line handling uses QCommandLineParser — `--help` and `--version` work, and any `--option` run is standalone and never forwarded to a running instance.
    Application-level services are now attached per WebEngine profile: the named "arora" profile gets the cookie jar, the custom scheme handlers, the download manager and the adblock interceptor at startup, and the off-the-record private profile picks up the same wiring the first time private browsing is used. Settings changes reapply to both profiles.
    Restored behavior that waited on the application object: clicking the private-browsing indicator exits private mode; Ctrl/Shift/Alt/middle-click in history and bookmark menus/toolbars opens entries in new or background tabs again; non-http navigations (mailto:, ftp:, ...) go through the recursion-guarded desktop handler; auth, proxy and SSL dialogs parent on the main window when one exists; language changes retranslate bookmarks and network settings.
    Settings and application data moved to the "Arora" application scope (the Qt 6 skeleton used lowercase "arora").

Translations, desktop integration and install rules (MIG16):
    All translation files are regenerated against the Qt 6 sources with lupdate-pro (the .pro-driven lupdate variant); five .ts files missing a `language` attribute (ast, ca, es, gl, pt_PT) are fixed so they update again. The Asturian translation keeps its correct `ast` tag — Qt 6 lupdate does not recognize it, so the file is updated under a temporary `es` tag and restored.
    `make install` now also installs the user-agent preset list (useragents.xml) into the package data directory — a file placed there overrides the compiled-in copy — and the 512x512 application icon. The .desktop file drops the deprecated "Application" category and registers x-scheme-handler/http(s) MIME types so desktops can offer Arora as a URL handler.
    The man page and desktop metadata are updated for the Qt 6 / WebEngine port (WebKit references and the dead Google Code bug URL are gone).

Qt 6 API sweep (MIG17):
    Last deprecated-API stragglers are gone: the View menu's Text Encoding submenu is populated from QStringConverter::availableCodecs() instead of Qt5Compat's QTextCodec, the network access manager's auth/proxy/SSL signal hookups use typed function-pointer connects, and remaining foreach loops, QMouseEvent::pos(), QAction::activate() and the Qt::CTRL shortcut modifier are converted to their Qt 6 forms. The whole tree now builds with zero compiler warnings under -Wall -Wextra.

Extensions (EXT01):
    Chrome Manifest-V3 extension support lands via Qt WebEngine's tech-preview QWebEngineExtensionManager: the Settings dialog gains an Extensions page that can load unpacked extension folders, install folders or .zip packages into the profile, toggle extensions on and off, and remove them. Installed extensions persist under the profile's data directory across sessions. Because Qt only ships a subset of the chrome.* API surface, Arora inspects manifest.json up front and warns which declared permissions are unsupported or only partially wired instead of letting extensions fail silently. Built-in component extensions (Chromium PDF viewer, Hangouts) are listed but can't be removed.
    A user-scripts path covers what extensions cannot: every *.js file dropped into the per-user "userscripts" data directory is injected into pages at document-ready (including private windows) and can be reloaded from the same Settings page.
    Known limits: Manifest V3 only (MV2 packages are rejected by Qt), extensions can't be loaded into off-the-record profiles, and the tech-preview API surfaces no per-extension permission prompt — Arora supplies its own consent step (see EXT02).
    Also fixed two latent teardown crashes exposed while verifying this: AdBlockSubscription tracked its in-flight download reply with a raw pointer that dangled on error paths (now QPointer), and the browser/autofill smokes now delete their windows before exit so WebEnginePages die before the profile is released.

Extension management UI (EXT02):
    Loading or installing an extension now always stops at a permission-review screen before anything reaches the engine: the dialog shows the package's name, version, source and description, translates its declared permissions and host permissions into plain-language access lines ("Read and change all your data on all websites", "Read and modify cookies", ...), and lists the support caveats Qt WebEngine has — unsupported chrome.* APIs called out as "will not work", partially-wired APIs as partial support, and an honest note that toolbar-action popups have no host UI. Manifest-V2 packages disable the approve button outright since Chromium rejects them, and Cancel is the default action so a stray Enter never installs anything.
    The Extensions page details pane now also shows the extension's source path and flags toolbar actions that cannot render.

Extension updates (EXT03):
    Extensions declaring a manifest "update_url" can now be checked for updates from the Extensions settings page: a "Check for Updates" button fetches each extension's gupdate XML manifest, compares dotted-quad versions, and downloads the offered package into a per-extension updates directory. Newer .zip packages are applied through the normal installer (a package carrying the same manifest "key" replaces the extension in place under the same id); packages Qt cannot install — typically .crx — are saved for manual install and reported as such. An opt-in "Automatically check for extension updates" checkbox (default off) enables a once-daily background check that detects and downloads but never installs silently — applying an update always comes from the manual button.
    Honest bounds: Chrome Web Store auto-update does not exist in QtWebEngine — there is no CRX3 signing verification and no store client — so only self-hosted update_url extensions can self-update; the details pane labels the rest "No update source". The plain GET gupdate protocol is supported; the Omaha POST variant used by update.googleapis.com is not attempted. Extensions without an update_url, or whose server is unreachable or answers an older/equal version, are reported accordingly and update failures never break browsing.

Private/Tor window extension policy (EXT04):
    Verified on Qt 6.12 that extensions genuinely cannot run in private windows: the engine refuses both loadExtension ("Can't load in off-the-record mode") and installExtension ("Cannot install in off-the-record mode") on off-the-record profiles, so there is no "allow in private windows" toggle — the Extensions page states the limit instead. Private windows still receive user scripts. Tor windows never get extensions at all: the tor profile is not even attached to the extension manager (extensions are a deanonymization surface there), the Extensions page in a Tor window says so, and the tor-window smoke asserts the refusal. `./arora --extension-otr-smoke` pins the engine rejection — if a future Qt lifts it, that smoke fails and the per-extension allow-in-private toggle should be built then.

Tools (TOOL01):
    Port the bundled command-line tools to Qt 6 and build them again with the browser. htmlToXBel no longer needs a web engine — Netscape bookmark HTML is scanned by a small forgiving parser and written out as XBEL, and bookmark folders marked FOLDED now export folded="true". arora-cacheinfo inspects Arora's application-level network disk cache under the standard cache location (QtWebEngine's own Chromium cache stays opaque). arora-placesimport imports Firefox places.sqlite visits into the current history store.

Test suite (TST01):
    The qtest suite builds and runs against Qt 6 again: `make check` (or autotests/runTests.sh) runs 27 test programs headless and reports failure honestly. The vendored ModelTest class was replaced by QtTest's QAbstractItemModelTester, tests that only exercised QtWebKit APIs are skipped (webPluginFactory/createPlugin), and the WebKit-only proxy tests (webpageproxy, networkaccessmanagerproxy) are dropped from the build since their modules no longer exist.
    Porting the tests surfaced three real fixes in the application: AdBlockManager::load() now skips stored subscriptions that duplicate an already-loaded one (the custom-rules subscription can exist before load runs), AdBlockSubscription correctly parses percent-encoded lastUpdate timestamps, and DownloadItem no longer lets two simultaneous downloads claim the same file name (previously they could overwrite each other — only on-disk files were deduplicated).

Coverage (COV01):
    `make check-coverage` measures test coverage for src/ using clang source-based instrumentation (lcov is not available): it builds an instrumented copy of the tree with `qmake -spec linux-clang CONFIG+=coverage`, runs the whole autotest suite and every --*-smoke check headless, and writes the totals plus a per-file table to .devin/COVERAGE.md. Runs are fully isolated from the user's real profile data. The first baseline stands at 62% of source lines; setting ARORA_COVERAGE_MIN=NN turns the target into a hard gate.

Coverage (COV02):
    Three new test programs raise coverage of the highest-value untested logic: tst_networkcookiejar exercises the application network cookie jar (domain matching including the two-level-domain blacklist, path/secure/expiry filtering, state save/restore), tst_cookiemodel covers the cookie and cookie-exception table models, and tst_bookmarksmodel covers the bookmarks tree model including drag-and-drop mime data and undo-stack edits. Overall src/ line coverage is now 65.8% — the cookie exception model is at 96%, the network cookie jar at 93%, the bookmarks model at 84%.

Coverage (COV03):
    Four new test programs plus a DownloadModel extension cover the WebEngine-adjacent logic end to end offscreen: tst_schemehandlers drives the arora-file, arora-resource and abp:subscribe custom scheme handlers through real page loads (directory listings, stub-resource canonicalization, the subscribe confirmation dialog), tst_adblockrequestinterceptor runs the profile's request interceptor against an in-process HTTP server (subresource block, @@ exception, $redirect to bundled stubs, $removeparam rewriting, main-frame refusal), tst_locationbar exercises the location bar's modifier suffixes, url tracking, Escape/focus-out restore, drag & drop, site icon and privacy indicator, and tst_webview covers zoom, javascript: urls, Ctrl+wheel zoom, navigation mouse buttons, url drops and asynchronous in-page find. src/ line coverage is now 69.4% — the adblock interceptor is at 96%, the arora-resource handler at 100%, the file-access handler at 90% and the location bar at 91%.

User agent (UA01):
    Arora no longer sends the QtWebEngine-badged user agent. Qt's factory string carries a "QtWebEngine/<version>" product token that bot detection fingerprints as automation — Google's /sorry/ interstitial fired on an ordinary google.com search. The default UA is now a vanilla Chrome string (Qt's own default minus that token, so the bundled Chromium version and platform stay accurate), applied to both the normal and the private browsing profile.
    The Tools > User Agent presets were a 2009 time capsule (IE 6-8, Opera 10/Presto, Safari 4, dead crawlers) and are refreshed to current Chrome/Edge/Firefox/Safari desktop and Android/iOS mobile entries plus current Googlebot/Bingbot. Custom override strings still work from the menu and now reach the off-the-record private profile too — the port previously wrote them to an unused WebEngine profile and cleared overrides by writing an empty user agent.

Coverage (COV04):
    Nine new test programs push src/ line coverage to 80.5% (was 69.4%) and `make check-coverage` now enforces 80% by default (ARORA_COVERAGE_MIN). The suite now exercises the assembled application end to end offscreen: BrowserApplication services and session save/restore, full BrowserMainWindow chrome (menus, dialog slots, state serialization, the close-multiple-tabs confirmation), the history/bookmark model-driven menus and toolbars, the settings dialog, the network access manager's auth/proxy/SSL dialogs against a local server, single-instance plumbing, and the toolbar search widget.
    Testing the dialogs surfaced two real fixes: OpenSearchEngineModel::flags() answered non-zero for invalid indexes (Qt 6's model tester aborts on that), and BrowserApplication::saveSession() never refreshed its in-memory copy of the saved session so "restore session" could report nothing to restore until the next launch.

Downloads hardening (SEC01):
    Server-suggested download file names are now fully sanitized before use: path components are stripped against both '/' and '\', "." and ".." names are rejected, control characters are removed, and overlong names are truncated while keeping the extension — a hostile Content-Disposition can no longer write outside the download directory.
    Files whose extension runs commands or installs software (.exe, .sh, .desktop, .msi, .jar, packages and friends) now raise a "may harm your computer" Keep/Discard prompt before anything is saved, and so do downloads whose declared content type is an executable or installer while the file name looks innocent (MIME/extension mismatch). Completed downloads are always written without the executable bit, so a kept script or .desktop file cannot execute when opened from a file manager.
    Removing a cancelled or interrupted download from the list now also deletes its partial file (QtWebEngine streams to "<name>.download" until completion); retrying a download drops the previous attempt's partial first, and existing files are still never silently overwritten.

URL scheme & external protocol hardening (SEC02):
    External protocol links (mailto:, ftp:, tel:, magnet:, and every other scheme the browser does not render itself) now raise a consent dialog before being handed to the desktop — a web page can no longer launch helper applications silently. Sub-frame requests for external protocols are dropped without prompting, and only one consent dialog can be open at a time so a redirect loop cannot stack modals.
    The internal "arora-file" directory-listing scheme is locked down: it is no longer registered as a secure context or CORS-enabled, and the handler refuses requests whose initiator is a remote page — a web site cannot read local directory listings through an iframe, subresource, or poked navigation. The "arora-resource" stub scheme likewise lost its unneeded local-file access.
    javascript: urls are no longer honored when they arrive through drag & drop, middle-click selection paste, or the single-instance IPC socket — those paths could have turned untrusted clipboard/peer content into script execution inside the current page. Typed and bookmarklet javascript: still works.
    Audit follow-up (SAFE05): every desktop hand-off path was re-checked — the consent gate covers arbitrary unregistered schemes (a blocked-popup probe page refuses them outright), typed omnibox input and redirect hops prompt like link clicks, and the remaining QDesktopServices/QProcess call sites are all user-initiated or post-consent. New autotest rows prove vendor schemes (magnet:, intent:, vnc:, sms:) prompt, custom-scheme deny launches nothing, and internal schemes (arora-resource, abp, view-source) navigate ungated.

Credential storage (SEC03):
    Saved passwords are no longer written to disk in plaintext. The autofill.dat store (form data including passwords) is now sealed with AES-256-GCM and the proxy password in the application settings is stored as a sealed value; legacy plaintext files and settings are read back transparently and migrated on the next save. The encryption key lives in securestore.key in the application data directory with owner-only permissions — the same guarantee Chromium provides on Linux without a desktop keyring. When the crypto backend is unavailable Arora refuses to write password forms back to disk instead of silently downgrading to plaintext. The full storage audit is in .devin/SEC03-report.md.

Untrusted-input parser hardening (SEC04):
    OpenSearch description documents are now capped at 1 MB, DTDs are rejected outright (removing the whole entity-expansion surface), and a document that ends abruptly inside an element no longer makes the reader spin on an exhausted parser. Remote engine descriptions, suggestion replies and engine icons are size-capped before parsing or decoding.
    Bookmark (XBEL) files are capped at 64 MB and folder/element nesting at 256 levels — a crafted document can no longer crash import via unbounded recursion, and oversized or truncated files report a parse error instead.
    Adblock subscriptions are bounded at 64 MB per file, 16 KB per line and 500k rules, and individual filters over 4 KB are kept inert. Filter rules written as raw regular expressions — including $removeparam patterns — are refused when the pattern is invalid or has the nested-quantifier shape that causes catastrophic backtracking, so a hostile list cannot stall page loads.
    The binary state files the browser reads back at startup (session restore, tab history, autofill, saved cookies, window layout) now bound serialized element counts against the bytes actually present before allocating, so a corrupt or hostile blob is rejected instead of triggering giant allocations. Extension manifests are capped at 1 MB.

Web permission broker (SEC05):
    Web pages can no longer touch sensitive features silently. Notifications, geolocation and mouse-lock requests now raise a consent dialog that names the requesting origin and the feature, with Deny as the default and an opt-in "Remember this decision" checkbox. Camera/microphone, screen capture, clipboard read/write, local-font enumeration and unknown features are denied outright without prompting.
    Remembered decisions are stored per security origin and per feature in the application settings, and a new "Site Permissions" list in the Privacy settings tab shows every remembered grant/deny with Remove and Clear All controls. Answers are always remembered for the session so a page cannot spam prompts; only checked "remember" answers are written to disk, and off-the-record pages never persist anything.
    The WebEngine profile is now pinned to AskEveryTime: previously Chromium persisted every granted/denied permission to permissions.json on disk on its own, bypassing both the consent prompt and the audit list. The surface-widening settings are pinned off as well: JavaScript clipboard access and paste, screen capture, local-content access to remote URLs, insecure content on secure pages and geolocation on insecure origins, and WebRTC is restricted to public interfaces.

Certificate-error interstitial (SEC06):
    A failed TLS certificate check now shows a proper warning page instead of a generic load failure. The interstitial names the failing site, explains the error and lists the certificate's subject, issuer, validity window, serial number and SHA-256 fingerprint so the user can judge what they are connecting to.
    "Back to safety" is the prominent action and returns to the previous page (or the start page when there is no history). "Proceed anyway (unsafe)" only appears for errors Chromium allows overriding — never for unoverridable failures such as HSTS-pinned hosts — and trusts just that host-and-certificate pair for the rest of the session. Nothing is written to a trust store: a new session, or a different profile, asks again.
    Certificate errors inside sub-frames are denied without disturbing the host page, and the interstitial itself is kept out of the browsing history.

Private browsing hardening (SEC07):
    Audited every application-side store against the off-the-record profile and closed the leaks found. In-flight private downloads are no longer written into the persisted download list — previously their url and save path reached QSettings through the auto-save. Private tabs are now skipped when the session is serialized, so quitting with a private tab open no longer stores its urls and page history into the restorable session. Closed private tabs no longer enter the Recently Closed Tabs list, which could reopen the private url in a recorded normal page. And searches typed into the toolbar search box while the target page is private are no longer added to the saved recent-searches list — the search box now follows the current tab's profile instead of a stale global flag.
    Verified end to end with a new autotest: private tabs really live on an off-the-record profile, private loads leave no history entry, cookies stay inside the throwaway private store, downloads/searches/tabs persist nothing, and the adblock interceptor still blocks content on private pages.

WebChannel bridge hardening (SEC08):
    The browser no longer trusts page-executed WebChannel calls. Saved-form reporting from the injected autofill script now carries a per-page-load random token that lives only inside the script's closure — a hostile page that loads qwebchannel.js and reaches the "aroraAutofill" object can no longer forge submitForm calls to poison the autofill store, and off-the-record pages never capture.
    Installing a search engine from a page (window.external.AddSearchProvider) now shows a consent dialog naming the calling site and only fetches the OpenSearch description after the user agrees — over http/https only; file: and other schemes are ignored — with the existing engine-confirmation dialog still guarding what gets installed. Only one prompt can be open at a time so a page cannot stack modals.
    The "arora" bridge object (translate/searchUrl) is now registered only while the page displays internal qrc: content such as the start page and is deregistered on navigation away. It no longer hands a live OpenSearchEngine object to JavaScript — pages see a plain currentEngineName string — and the start page finally loads qwebchannel.js again, making its search box and engine display work for the first time since the WebEngine port.

URL input perimeter (SEC09):
    Command-line url arguments are now treated as untrusted input like drops, pastes and forwarded second-instance messages: `arora javascript:...` is refused instead of executing the payload in the new window, and the check runs on the resolved url so padding tricks cannot slip a script through. Unknown schemes continue to reach the consent-gated external-protocol prompt introduced earlier.
    The external download program option (Settings > Downloads) now only hands http/https/ftp urls to the configured command — file:, data:, blob:, javascript: and Arora's internal schemes are refused and fall back to the internal downloader. The url travels as exactly one percent-encoded argv element, so it cannot smuggle extra arguments into the handler.
    The single-instance socket moved out of the shared temp dir into a user-only location (the runtime dir, or a 0700 per-user directory as fallback) and is created with user-only access bits at bind time — other local accounts can no longer connect to it and push urls into the running browser.

Chrome markup injection (SEC10):
    Page-controlled strings can no longer inject markup into browser chrome. Qt widgets auto-detect HTML by default (Qt::AutoText), so a page title like "<img src=x onerror=...>" previously rendered as markup in tab tooltips, history menus, download items and dialogs. A new SafeText helper (src/utils/safetext.h) now handles every such sink: labels and message boxes that take web text use Qt::PlainText, tooltips and other AutoText contexts use a reliably-escaped form, and menu/action text is sanitized so '&' can't become a hidden mnemonic and a tab can't fake a shortcut column.
    A PlainTextItemDelegate is installed on every view fed web-controlled models — history/bookmarks/download/autofill/cookie/search-engine trees and the location-bar and search-suggestion completer popups — so model strings are always painted literally. Tab tooltips, back/forward and recently-closed history menus, the download list, search suggestions, "Add '<engine>'" entries, adblock subscribe and external-application prompts, and user-agent menu entries are all covered by the audit.

Search suggestion privacy (SEC11):
    Search suggestions are now off by default: typing in the search box previously streamed every keystroke to the current engine's suggest endpoint without asking. Suggestions are a per-engine opt-in — enable them for the current engine from the new "Search Suggestions" group on the Privacy settings tab (the label names the engine the keystrokes would go to), from the checkable "Search Suggestions" entry in the search-button's engines menu, or per engine via the new "Suggestions" column in the OpenSearch manager dialog. With the flag off the suggestion data path issues no network requests at all.
    Accept-Language audit: when no language list is configured the header no longer falls back to the full system locale list (QLocale::uiLanguages); it now sends just the process locale and its base language, matching what other browsers send. Configured lists (the accept-language dialog) are unchanged.

Clear private data completeness (SEC12):
    The "Clear Private Data" dialog now really removes DOM storage — a new "Site Data" checkbox (on by default) wipes localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB, service workers, WebSQL/File System data, blobs, quota bookkeeping, trust tokens and persisted HSTS/network state. Qt WebEngine exposes no DOM-storage clear API (clearHttpCache only reaches the HTTP cache — exactly the data trackers use), so the clear first sweeps every open page on the profile with a storage-clearing script — an emptied storage area cannot be re-flushed from Chromium's in-memory copy — then schedules the on-disk storage trees for removal at the next profile startup (deleting them under the running browser wedges Chromium's storage services, found during HARD01), which recreates them empty.
    The profile storage directory and everything under it are kept owner-only (0700 directories / 0600 files): repaired at startup and after each site-data wipe. One honest limit: storage belonging to origins with no open page stays on disk until the deferred wipe at the next browser start, and can be re-read if the site is revisited in the same session.

AdBlock matcher scaling (PERF02):
    Ad-block request matching is now indexed instead of scanning every rule per request. With an EasyList-size list (~60k rules) a single resource lookup cost ~500ms before — enough to stall every page load; it now answers in under a millisecond (~550x faster on the benchmark corpus). Matching results are unchanged: the index only decides which rules could possibly match, and a differential test compares every decision against the original linear scan.

Parser fuzzing (FUZZ01):
    Added a libFuzzer harness (clang, AddressSanitizer + UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer) covering every parser that consumes hostile input: AdBlock rules, OpenSearch XML, XBEL bookmarks, the serialized history file, cookie-jar state blobs, bounded QDataStream list decoding, SecureStore sealed blobs and the htmlToXBel importer. Run it with `make check-fuzz` (or .devin/run-fuzz.sh <seconds>); seed corpora and dictionaries live under fuzz/. Two code paths were extracted so the fuzzers drive real production code: history-file decoding moved out of HistoryManager::load() into HistoryParser, and htmlToXBel's conversion core moved out of main() into a callable converter.

Sanitizer sweep (HARD01):
    Added an AddressSanitizer+UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer build variant (CONFIG+=sanitize under clang, or CONFIG+=sanitize-strict for fatal UBSan) and a `make check-sanitize` gate that builds an instrumented copy of the tree, runs the whole autotest suite and every --*-smoke check offscreen, and reports findings in .devin/SANITIZER.md (leak detection is off by default; ARORA_SANITIZE_LEAKS=1 opts in). The sweep caught real bugs now fixed: BookmarkNode::add could pass an out-of-range offset to QList::insert (Qt 6 extends the list with uninitialized slots instead of clamping), two BrowserMainWindow booleans were read uninitialized when saving fullscreen state, smoke-check lambdas captured block-local state that died before exec() ran them, and a bookmarks autotest dereferenced a node after deleting it.
    The sweep also exposed a Clear Private Data hazard: deleting Chromium's live storage trees under a running browser can wedge the storage services so the next navigation hangs outright (bisected: Local Storage, IndexedDB, Service Worker and WebStorage removals each break on their own). Site-data clearing therefore keeps only the in-session page sweep and drops a sentinel file — the on-disk storage trees are removed at the next profile startup, before any page exists. A navigation that races the clear path's async profile work can also lose its transaction (the load hangs until retried); the window is subhuman in real use and self-heals on reload.

Static-analysis sweep (STAT01):
    Added a `make check-static` sweep that builds a throwaway copy of the tree with GCC's -fanalyzer on every translation unit, then replays each compile line through the clang static analyzer (deduplicated to one run per source file). The full deduplicated output and per-finding triage are committed to .devin/STAT01-report.md and .devin/STAT01-triage.md. The sweep found zero actionable bugs: every in-tree finding is a known analyzer limitation around Qt — QPointer guards and connect() context-object lifetimes it cannot see, throwing operator new modeled as fallible, std::function payloads Qt owns and frees, and '<unknown>' uninitialized-value noise inside implicitly-shared internals. clang-tidy/cppcheck/scan-build are unavailable on this machine (no sudo), so the sweep is g++ -fanalyzer + clang++ --analyze.

Memory-leak sweep (MEM01):
    Added a `make check-leaks` gate (.devin/check-leaks.sh) that runs the autotest suite and every --*-smoke check under LeakSanitizer (detect_leaks=1 on the ASan build), then reruns the suite under valgrind memcheck (QT_ENABLE_REGEXP_JIT=0 — pcre2's JIT floods memcheck with false positives). Every reported leak is classified by allocation site: the gate fails on anything allocated in Arora code and records upstream Qt/WebEngine/system noise in .devin/LEAKS.md. Suppression files: .devin/lsan.supp (leaf third-party libraries only — LSan matches suppressions against every stack frame, so Qt-wide patterns would hide our own leaks) and .devin/valgrind.supp.
    Leaks fixed: every DownloadManager leaked its DownloadModel — its constructor's first argument is a manager back-pointer, not a QObject parent (now `new DownloadModel(this, this)`); the adblock and file-access scheme handlers passed unparented QBuffers to reply jobs (now parented to the job); smoke-check counters in main.cpp allocated with raw `new int` were never freed (now std::shared_ptr captured by the lambdas that use them); and two autotests leaked objects they created — an unparented HistoryCompletionModel and OpenSearchEngines rejected by addEngine() (now ownership-guarded).
    Re-verified under Qt 6.12.0 with a bounded, per-binary run (.devin/MEM02-report.md): 12,256 LeakSanitizer reports across the suite and every smoke check, all allocated inside upstream QtWebEngine/udev/fontconfig code — zero in Arora code. A valgrind pass over the small non-WebEngine tests likewise reports zero definite or indirect leaks. The follow-up fix pass closed with an empty fix list — nothing allocated in Arora code remained to fix, and no new suppressions were needed.

C++ modernization sweep (CPP01):
    Brought the Qt4-era codebase up to modern C++ practice across src/, tools/ and autotests/: every overriding method is now marked `override` (200 sites), every literal `0` used as a null pointer is `nullptr` (329 sites — `*parent = 0` defaults included), unambiguous owning raw pointers became QScopedPointer/std::unique_ptr (DownloadManager's icon provider, the adblock-rust engine, the bookmarks root node), `Q_DISABLE_COPY` became `Q_DISABLE_COPY_MOVE`, trivial special members became `= default`, and value-type setters that store their argument take it by value and `std::move` it. `[[nodiscard]]` now marks the owned-pointer factories (XbelReader/OpenSearchReader::read, AdBlockRustEngine::create) and the download security helpers. The whole tree now compiles under -Wsuggest-override and -Wzero-as-null-pointer-constant with zero warnings, on top of the existing -Wall -Wextra.

Zero-warning gate (WRN01):
    The deprecation warnings are now pinned in the build configuration (-Wdeprecated/-Wdeprecated-declarations on gcc, -Wdeprecated-warnings/-Wdeprecated-declarations on clang) alongside the -Wall -Wextra that warn_on already supplied, and the whole shipping tree compiles with zero diagnostics. `make check` now runs a check-warnings gate first — it builds a throwaway copy of the tree and fails before any test runs if the compiler emits even one warning or error while compiling the shipping code. No -Werror was added to the shipped configuration so distro/packaging builds are unaffected.

Event-loop blocking audit (FRZ01):
    Removed every GUI-thread blocking wait found by the sweep. The single-instance server no longer freezes: a client that connects but never writes used to stall the whole browser inside waitForReadyRead() — connections are now serviced asynchronously off readyRead and idle sockets are reaped. The IPC write waits are bounded (2s). Importing Netscape-format bookmark HTML no longer spawns the htmlToXBel tool and blocks on waitForFinished — the same parser now runs in-process, which also fixes the feature in installed builds (the tool's binary was never installed, only its man page).

Accessibility audit (A11Y01):
    Screen readers now have a name for every chrome control that used to be silent: the private-browsing indicator is a real button (focusable, named, keyboard-activatable to leave private mode) instead of a mouse-only label, and the site icon, clear/search buttons, address and search fields, tab strip, and download rows all expose names and descriptions. Dialog labels were wired to their inputs as buddies so fields announce as "User name", "Password", "Home Page", and so on, with mnemonics added where missing. A new autotests/accessibility suite pins the contract, and the AT-SPI bridge is verified attaching on Linux (QAccessible::isActive() reports true once a widget is shown under an a11y bus).

Performance sanity (PERF01):
    Cold start no longer stalls for seconds when adblock lists are populated: a cached EasyList-size ruleset cost ~6.7s inside profile bring-up (browser ready in ~0.6s now) because every parsed filter eagerly compiled a QRegularExpression that mostly never fires. Compilation is deferred to first match through a shared, lock-guarded pattern cache — per-rule lazy flags would race because request matching runs on the WebEngine IO thread while the GUI thread can scan the same rules — and the wildcard-to-regexp converter no longer recompiles its five helper expressions per filter. History loading appends then reverses instead of prepending each entry, which Qt 6's contiguous QList made quadratic. The startup permission sweep over the profile tree now runs once per launch and only chmods files whose permissions actually differ. A headless `./arora --perf-smoke` reports cold-start, first-window, tab-open, large-history and permission-sweep timings.

Self-contained Linux bundle (PACK01):
    `make bundle` now produces a relocatable directory (dist/Arora-<version>-linux-<arch>/) that contains everything Arora needs to run on a system with no Qt installed: the binary, every Qt and ICU shared library it links, QtWebEngineProcess plus the webengine .pak/icudtl resources, the platform and support plugins (including the Wayland client platform, shell integrations and xdg-desktop-portal theme), Qt and Arora translations, and the freedesktop desktop file/icons/man page. A qt.conf next to the binary anchors Qt's search paths at the bundle root and a thin ./arora wrapper sets the library path, so the directory can be unpacked anywhere.
    Translations and the useragents.xml override are found in a bundle too — installedDataDirectory() and the language manager now prefer the <bindir>/../share/arora layout before the compile-time prefix.
    Verified in a bubblewrap sandbox with the development Qt installation masked: the bundle starts and loads pages under the offscreen platform and natively under Wayland against a minimal headless compositor (.devin/wayland-compositor.c), exercised by `make check-bundle`.
    The desktop entry gained StartupWMClass, Keywords and translated Keywords entries. On X11 note Qt 6 still needs the system libxcb-cursor0 package for the xcb platform plugin; Wayland needs nothing beyond the bundle.

License audit (LIC01):
    Added THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES at the repository root documenting every bundled or linked component and its license: the Trolltech Qt-demo code base Arora started from (GPL-2/3 + GPL Exception 1.2), the BSD-licensed src/utils/ classes, the MPL/GPL/LGPL tri-licensed network cookie jar, the GPL-2.0 Arora icon by Jens Bache-Wiig, the optional Rust adblock engine's crate dependencies (adblock 0.13.3 MPL-2.0; the rest MIT/Apache-2.0/Unicode-3.0/Unlicense), the runtime-fetched EasyList/uBlock subscription licenses, Qt 6/QtWebEngine/OpenSSL as non-vendored runtime dependencies, and the community translations. `make bundle` now ships the notices file alongside the GPL texts in dist/.

Post-migration sweep (AUD01):
    Removed the last Qt4-era leftovers: the orphan autotest dirs for the deleted WebPageProxy/NetworkAccessManagerProxy modules, the Qt-4.5-era buildosx.sh packaging script, builddeb.sh (its debian-upstream/ directory is gone), the dead `#if QT_VERSION` guard in ClearButton, the OS/2 qmake scopes in the tool projects, and the WebKit-era -webkit-gradient/-webkit-border-radius CSS in the start page (now standard linear-gradient/border-radius). The About dialog's version label is renamed off its old "webkitVersion" widget name, and the last headers used but never listed in project files (streamingutils.h, autotests/qtry.h) are listed now.

Session restore (SESS01):
    Closing and relaunching with "Restore windows and tabs from last time" selected restores the saved session end to end again. The saved current tab is now applied after the restored tabs exist — it used to be set before them, which was a no-op against the single placeholder tab and left every restored session on the first tab. A tab whose serialized page history fails to parse now falls back to its flat saved url instead of being silently dropped, which also lets sessions written by the QtWebKit build restore their tabs (old per-tab history blobs have a different layout; WebEngine cannot inject a back/forward stack either way). The crash-loop guard ("Arora crashed while trying to restore this session") now arms only after the session blob validates, so a corrupt or stale blob no longer replays the prompt on every launch.
    New headless `./arora --session-smoke` and `./arora --restore-smoke` options script the real close -> relaunch cycle — three tabs with the middle one current — and verify url order, the restored current index, that the pages actually load, and that private-window tabs never reach the saved session blob.

Tab settings (TABS01):
    The settings dialog's "Tabs" page is now named "Tab Settings" and gains a "Tab bar position" selector (Top/Bottom/Left/Right), saved to the existing tabs settings group and applied live to every open window as well as to newly created ones. On Left/Right the tab strip runs vertically — tabs stay movable, the context menu and close buttons keep working, and dragging a tab away from the strip (sideways, not upward) still tears it out; the corner "+" and shared close buttons are hidden in the vertical positions because QTabWidget does not lay corner widgets out there — New Tab remains on the File menu, the Ctrl+T-class shortcut, and the tab bar's context menu and empty-area double-click.

Tor daemon (TOR01):
    Arora can now launch and manage its own Tor daemon as a child process — the foundation for private windows that route through Tor. The manager prefers a system `tor` (PATH, /usr/bin/tor or the ARORA_TOR_BINARY override) and falls back to a bundled Tor Expert Bundle (BuildProcess/fetch-tor.sh downloads the official per-platform bundle into the package data area; no binaries are committed). Tor runs with a private data directory, a loopback SOCKS5 listener and control port on auto-assigned ports, and cookie authentication; the browser takes control-protocol ownership, tracks bootstrap progress to 100% before declaring the proxy ready, and shuts the daemon down cleanly when the app exits — it never runs as root. `./arora --tor-smoke` verifies the whole path end to end against a real daemon.

Tor window (TOR02):
    File → "New Tor Window" (or `arora --tor`) opens a dedicated Tor browsing window as a separate Arora process — QtWebEngine's proxy is process-global, so Tor routing cannot be a per-tab feature. The window owns its own managed daemon, arms a fail-closed SOCKS5 proxy (127.0.0.1:1) until bootstrap completes so nothing leaks onto clearnet, and then routes every tab in the window through the discovered SOCKS listener. Browsing uses a dedicated off-the-record profile — memory-only cache, no history, cookies, storage or autofill persisted — and is hardened: no extensions, no WebChannel bridge objects, autofill and search suggestions forced off, the user's configured clearnet proxy ignored, and plain-http requests upgraded to https except on .onion hosts. A violet toolbar accent, a "Tor" badge and a "(Tor)" title marker keep Tor windows unmistakable; statusbar messages track bootstrap. Normal windows are untouched. `./arora --tor-window-smoke` verifies the whole contract end to end — fail-closed arm, OTR profile, and `check.torproject.org/api/ip` answering IsTor:true through both the application fetch manager and the WebEngine page stack.

Look and feel (UIP01):
    Arora now follows the desktop light/dark preference (QStyleHints::colorScheme, Qt 6.5+): on desktops that report a dark scheme but ship a widget style that cannot darken (Windows, macOS, plain Fusion) the browser installs a Fusion-based dark palette, and it tracks live preference changes via colorSchemeChanged. ARORA_COLOR_SCHEME=dark|light overrides the desktop preference for systems that do not report one. Chrome colors that fought dark themes were fixed — the download item's info text uses the palette's placeholder color instead of hardcoded gray and the search-field magnifier glyph tracks the palette instead of painting darkGray.
    The cramped Qt4-era metrics were relaxed: the navigation toolbar gains margins and item spacing, the address bar and toolbar search share a taller omnibox height, the bookmarks bar gets modest padding, and tabs carry a minimum thickness.
    The Clear Private Data dialog indents its item list under the heading and pins the buttons to the bottom, and a stale fixed-width spacer in the Preferences downloads group no longer forces the page's minimum width. Fractional-scaling fix: the search button now renders its glyph at the device pixel ratio instead of a fixed 12x16 image that blurred on 1.25x/1.5x displays.
    Icon artwork and theme wiring are untouched by design.

Omnibox address bar (SRCH01):
    The address bar is now a real omnibox: input that is not address-shaped searches the default engine instead of guessing a host. Multi-word text ("browser test") and bare single words ("word") go to the search engine — the old behavior silently resolved them as http:// URLs. Address-shaped input still navigates: explicit schemes load as typed, and single tokens navigate when they are localhost/*.localhost, host:port (localhost:8080, [::1]:8080), or dotted names (docs.qt.io, 127.0.0.1, a.b). 'keyword terms' OpenSearch shortcuts keep first priority. The "use the default search engine as fallback" setting is now on by default; unchecking it restores the old bare-http guess.
    The address bar's completion dropdown can now show live search suggestions above the history matches (capped at five, labeled with the engine name). Suggestions are governed by the same per-engine opt-in introduced for the search box — nothing is sent to a suggest endpoint unless the engine is explicitly enabled in its menu or in Settings, and the Tor window never suggests. Picking a suggestion routes it through the same guess logic, so a url-shaped suggestion navigates and anything else searches.

Search settings (SRCH02):
    The preferences dialog gains a dedicated "Search" page: a default-engine combo listing every OpenSearch engine (the pick applies on OK), a "Manage..." button opening the existing engine editor for add/remove/restore-defaults, the moved "use the default search engine as fallback" checkbox (same settings key, existing preference preserved) and the per-engine "Search Suggestions" opt-in moved over from the Privacy page. The suggestions checkbox now follows whichever engine the combo displays — edits for several engines queue up and are written together on OK — so it stays honest while browsing engines that are not yet the default.

Search box visibility (SRCH03):
    The dedicated search box next to the address bar is now opt-in: the "Show dedicated search box" checkbox on the General preferences page is off by default, since the omnibox address bar covers searching already. The widget stays constructed while hidden — the toggle applies live to every open window — and the Web Search shortcut (Ctrl+K) focuses the address bar instead of an invisible widget while the box is off.

User agent (UA02):
    The browser's UA client hints (Sec-CH-UA*) now agree with the vanilla-Chrome user agent: they previously branded the engine as bare "Chromium" while the UA string claimed "Chrome/<version>" — real Chrome sends a "Google Chrome" brand alongside, and the mismatch was a bot-detection tell. The hints are aligned on every profile (browsing, private and Tor) and re-aligned whenever a custom user agent is applied; a non-Chrome override resets them to the honest Chromium defaults.
    When Google does divert a search to its /sorry/ "unusual traffic" bot check, the page now carries a readable banner explaining that the connection was rate-limited (not an Arora error) and pointing at the default-engine picker under Tools > Options > Search. Google's own recovery flow is left intact underneath — with JavaScript on, the challenge usually solves transparently and lands back on the search results.
    `./arora --sorry-smoke` is a diagnostic that reproduces the whole flow on a fresh profile: it dumps the wire request headers (client hints included), runs a control fetch through the application network manager for the same URL/UA/IP, and reports whether a /sorry/ landing is browser-side or IP reputation.

Button chrome (UIP02):
    Tab close buttons now behave like every modern browser: the ✕ lives on the tab itself but only appears on the current tab and the one under the cursor, instead of Qt's always-visible indicator on every tab. The single shared corner close button remains available through the existing "oneCloseButton" setting.
    Toolbar buttons are consistent across platforms: the navigation bar pins its 18px icon size everywhere (it was macOS-only, so other platforms fell back to the style's larger default), and the bookmarks bar is pinned to 16px favicon size. Buttons keep their flat-with-hover look — nothing forces the raised Qt4-era frames.
    Dialog buttons get uniform modern metrics: a minimum width so short captions like "OK" stop collapsing, and a taller minimum height, applied to every dialog's push buttons through size constraints — the native style keeps rendering them, so the look follows the active light/dark theme. Compact composite rows (the per-download controls) are exempt.

Icon sets (ICONS01):
    All chrome icons now resolve through a central AroraIcon::get(name) helper that speaks freedesktop icon names: it asks the running desktop theme first, then falls back to bundled sets. A new "Icons:" selector on the General preferences page offers Native (follow the desktop theme), Adwaita, Breeze and Tabler, applied live to every open window.
    Adwaita, Breeze and Tabler ship as proper icon themes inside the Qt resource system (35 mapped actions each, light + recolored dark variants since Qt does not repaint monochrome SVGs for dark palettes). Anything a set lacks inherits the chain above it, and the original 2009 artwork stays as the final fallback — plus it repairs a dead ":addbookmark.png" reference that rendered as a blank icon.
    `./arora --icons-smoke` verifies coverage, live switching, light/dark glyph contrast and the fallback chain headlessly; the icon set choice is persisted under MainWindow/iconTheme.

Privacy hardening (PRIV01):
    A new "Connections & Storage" group on the Privacy preferences page collects five toggles, all defaulting to the private choice where that is safe. Plain-http page loads are upgraded to https when a secure endpoint plausibly exists — loopback, LAN/private-IP and .onion/.local hosts are skipped, and a site whose secure connection fails is allowed over plain http again for the rest of the session with the error page explaining the downgrade. Navigations to a different site carry the destination's own origin as Referer so the referring page is never leaked (subresources fall back to Chromium's origin-only policy — the header is composed after request interceptors run, an honest QtWebEngine bound).
    WebRTC leak protection is now configurable and tightened: beyond the existing public-interfaces-only attribute, the Chromium flag --force-webrtc-ip-handling-policy=disable_non_proxied_udp is appended to QTWEBENGINE_CHROMIUM_FLAGS at startup so ICE transports only ever run through the configured proxy. An opt-in secure-DNS toggle enables Chromium's DNS-over-HTTPS auto-upgrade (known-resolver list only — a custom endpoint is not exposed). Third-party cookies are rejected by default in the cookie filter even under the permissive accept policy, while the per-site allow/block/session exceptions keep their precedence.
    An opt-in "Clear browsing data when Arora exits" setting performs the full private-data wipe at quit — history, cookies, site storage, caches and the saved session — and drops a sentinel so Chromium's on-disk storage trees are removed at the next start rather than under a running browser, where deleting them would wedge Chromium's storage services.
    `./arora --privacy-smoke` verifies the pipeline end to end on loopback (un-upgraded http load, referer spoofed on the cross-site navigation, first-party cookie kept and third-party cookie rejected); autotests/privacy covers the upgrade candidacy rules, downgrade set, cookie policy, deferred-wipe sentinels and flag composition. What an observer can still see — destination IPs, timing, the usual fingerprinting surface — is documented in the README; the Tor window remains the answer for hiding traffic endpoints.

Qt 6.12 toolchain (QTUP01):
    The browser now builds and runs against Qt 6.12.0 (user-local install at ~/Qt/6.12.0/gcc_64 via .devin/qt-env.sh). Qt 6.11.3 stays on disk as the rollback path; the bundle and analysis helpers' QTDIR defaults, the runner prompt and the task-file context follow the new toolchain.
    The only source-level change 6.12 required is in the autotests: QDragEnterEvent/QDragMoveEvent constructors taking QPoint are newly deprecated in favor of QPointF. The custom user-agent machinery is unaffected — the default UA is derived from the live factory string at runtime, so it follows whatever Chromium version the Qt build reports.

Site privacy panel (SHLD01):
    A shield button in the location bar now opens a per-site privacy panel under the indicator: the page's security state (secure https / insecure http / certificate error / local content), a cookie control that sets a per-site Allow / Block / Allow-for-session exception plus the count of cookies the site has stored, a "Block ads and trackers on this site" toggle, the remembered permission grants for the origin each with a Revoke button, and a "Clear site data" action that removes the site's cookies and sweeps its live DOM storage.
    The content toggle writes a standard @@||host^$document exception into the custom adblock rules, so unchecking it exempts the site from both network blocking and element hiding; the shield icon greys out while a site is whitelisted. Cookie exceptions and adblock rules persist immediately. Clearing site data wipes the live page's storage areas; Chromium's on-disk storage trees still follow the deferred next-start removal from the site-data wipe.

Credential storage hardening (SEC13):
    Saved passwords can now be protected by a master passphrase (Settings > Privacy > Saved Passwords > "Set Master Passphrase..."). With a passphrase set, the AES-256-GCM key is derived in memory by a bundled pure-C Argon2id KDF (64 MiB / 3 passes / 4 lanes, parameters from RFC 9106) and never written to disk — only a salt, the KDF parameters and an authenticated verifier live in securestore.kdf. A wrong passphrase is rejected by the AES-GCM tag on the verifier, so no password oracle leaks; the store prompts to unlock on first credential access and can be re-locked any time from Tools > Lock Credential Store.
    Changing or removing the passphrase re-encrypts the stored data; disabling returns to the default random key file (0600 permissions — note that mode protects the ciphertext from other users and offline reads, but not the key file itself). All key material is zeroized on lock and on exit. No OS keychain, libsecret, DPAPI or Keychain dependency is used — the same code path builds on Linux, Windows and macOS.

Security levels (SECLVL):
    A "Security Level" selector at the top of the Privacy preferences page offers Mullvad-style tiers. Standard (the default) is the current behavior. Safer blocks script-execution fetches — external scripts, workers and service workers — on pages loaded over insecure http, and media only plays after a click (PlaybackRequiresUserGesture). Safest disables JavaScript on every site through the profile settings on top of click-to-play media; the "Enable Javascript" checkbox is greyed out while it is selected and its stored value is restored when the tier comes back down.
    The http script block runs in the profile's request interceptor — also on the Tor profile, where .onion is the only http left. http pages on loopback keep their scripts because Chromium treats them as secure contexts; plain-http LAN and public hosts do not. Honest bound: the interceptor cannot reach inline <script> blocks, so Safer covers external script fetches only — a fully scriptless page is Safest's job. `./arora --seclvl-smoke` verifies all tiers end to end against a local http fixture.

Fingerprint normalization (PRIV02):
    A new "Fingerprint Resistance" group on the Privacy preferences page carries two opt-in toggles. "Report UTC as the timezone" forces the TZ environment to UTC before the web engine initializes — every Date and Intl read in web pages then reports UTC regardless of the system zone, and switching the toggle off restores the caller's own TZ exactly as inherited (engine processes already running keep their zone until restart). "Report generic language preferences" sends "en-US, en" as the Accept-Language header and navigator.languages on both the browsing profile and the application's own network fetches, while the configured language list is preserved underneath and used again when the toggle is off. The Tor window's profile normalizes unconditionally — a localized language list next to its vanilla user agent would be a per-user fingerprint either way.
    User-agent consistency is verified end to end: the UA sent on the wire, navigator.userAgent, navigator.platform and the client-hints brand list all tell the same vanilla-Chrome story with no QtWebEngine token. Deliberately not faked (QtWebEngine exposes no hooks): canvas/WebGL fingerprinting and hardware-concurrency spoofing. `./arora --fingerprint-smoke` checks the in-process wiring and respawns itself with the toggles persisted to assert what JavaScript actually sees.

Zero telemetry (TELEM01):
    A clean launch no longer makes any outbound connection the user did not ask for. The seeded adblock subscriptions (EasyList, EasyPrivacy, uBlock filters) previously auto-downloaded on first run and again whenever they went stale; remote list downloads are now consent-gated — a one-time prompt on first launch asks whether to fetch them, and consent is also granted by explicitly enabling the blocker, pressing "Update Subscription", or accepting an abp: subscribe link. Until then the blocker simply runs with no remote rules; lists already downloaded keep working.
    Chromium's own silent channels are suppressed via QTWEBENGINE_CHROMIUM_FLAGS applied before the engine latches them: --disable-background-networking, --disable-component-update, --disable-domain-reliability, --disable-metrics, --disable-sync and --no-first-run. No update-checker or phone-home code exists in the tree, and OpenSearch suggestions remain opt-in per engine.
    `./arora --telemetry-smoke` performs the verification headlessly: it points both the Qt fetch stack and the engine at a loopback capture proxy (live before the application constructor, so even startup-early fetchers are caught), polls /proc for process-owned sockets that might bypass the proxy, and fails if anything reaches out within 30 seconds.
    Fixed as a side effect: downloaded filter lists were written to a still-open QFile and then re-read as empty, so remote subscriptions never actually persisted.

Per-site JavaScript control (JSCTL):
    JavaScript can now be allowed or blocked per site. The shield panel's new "JavaScript" selector offers Site default / Always allow / Always block for the current host, and a slim bar across the top of a page whose scripts were blocked — "Scripts blocked on <host>" — offers Allow once (session-scoped) and Always allow (persisted). Rules live in their own settings map rather than the SEC05 permission broker, which is keyed on QWebEnginePermission types and has no JavaScript feature; off-the-record pages never write the persistent store, so private-session "always" lasts only until the browser closes.
    The decision is applied through each page's own QWebEngineSettings::JavascriptEnabled before the navigation commits, so two tabs can disagree and the choice composes with the security tiers: an explicit allow grant beats every automatic rule — a granted site runs JavaScript even under Safest, and its external script fetches are exempted from Safer's http block inside the request interceptor. A block kills scripts even under Standard. A parent-domain rule covers subdomains and the most specific matching rule wins. The shield icon gains a small badge while scripts are blocked, and the persistent grants are auditable (and revocable) in Settings > Privacy > Site Permissions.
    autotests/scriptcontrol covers the rule store (normalization, session overlay, persistence, specificity), the full tier/rule/global-preference decision table, the interceptor grant exemption, and four end-to-end cases against an in-process http server — including the infobar's allow buttons reloading the page and the Safest-plus-grant combination.

Default search engine (DENG01):
    The compiled-in default search engine is now DuckDuckGo instead of Google. A bundled DuckDuckGo OpenSearch description joins the engine set (search via https://duckduckgo.com/?q=, suggestions via the duckduckgo.com/ac endpoint). Only profiles that never picked an engine are affected — a saved choice is always respected — and Google stays bundled and selectable like before. As a bonus, default-engine searches no longer hit Google's /sorry/ bot-check, so a fresh install gets working search out of the box.

Startup and menu responsiveness (PERF03):
    Startup no longer stalls inside the application constructor: profile bring-up used to build the adblock matcher's rules snapshot synchronously — a full parse of every subscribed filter list — and built it twice. The interceptor now installs with its first snapshot deferred to a queued warmup that runs once the startup navigation is already dispatched; measured on a real profile the constructor dropped ~750→~200ms and first-window-shown ~755→~590ms, with the rules parse (~500ms total) running while the first page is in flight (requests in between see an empty ruleset, i.e. allow-all — documented tradeoff).
    Menus backed by large models (history, bookmarks) no longer rebuild their whole action tree on every open: ModelMenu repopulates only when the model or a populate-time input changed since the last show — a benchmarked 10,000-entry menu went from ~90ms to ~0ms on reopen while the first build is unchanged.
    New `./arora --profile-startup` option (or ARORA_PROFILE_STARTUP=1) prints a millisecond timeline of the startup stages to stderr, and `--perf-smoke` additionally reports populated-history and flat-menu open/reopen latency.

Custom DNS-over-HTTPS (DOH01):
    Qt 6's native QWebEngineGlobalSettings::setDnsMode (available since Qt WebEngine 6.6) turns out to expose exactly what the earlier privacy pass concluded was unreachable, so the "Secure DNS" checkbox is now a mode selector: Off (system resolver), Automatic upgrade for resolvers on Chromium's known-provider list, Custom server with system fallback, and Custom server in strict mode — strict fails resolution rather than letting a lookup leak out over plain DNS when the DoH endpoint is down. The endpoint field defaults to https://cloudflare-dns.com/dns-query and accepts any https:// DoH URI template.
    The legacy privacy/secureDns boolean is migrated (enabled → Automatic) and kept in sync, and the mode applies live — no restart needed — through the QSettings -> applySecureDns() path. Honest bound documented in the README: CNAME-cloaked trackers are still not uncloaked, because that needs DNS-response inspection and QtWebEngine exposes no DNS-response hook to the adblock engine.
    `./arora --doh-smoke` verifies the wiring headlessly in three stages: strict mode with a dead loopback endpoint must fail every resolution (proves the setting gates DNS and does not fall back), strict mode against a live resolver must load a page (under SecureOnly the resolution could only have come from DoH), and switching back to Off must restore system-DNS loads at runtime.

About dialog (ABOUT01):
    The About dialog now credits the Qt 6 port maintainer alongside the original author: a second copyright line "Copyright © 2026-2027 Janos Szenfner" sits under Benjamin C. Meyer's 2007-2010 credit (left intact), followed by a clickable link to the current repository (https://github.com/janos-szenfner/arora). The AUTHORS file — shown by the dialog's Authors button — now lists Janos Szenfner first.

Web Inspector (DVT01):
    Tools > Web Inspector and the context menu's "Inspect Element" work again — they were silent no-ops because Qt WebEngine renders Chromium DevTools into a page bound via setDevToolsPage(), and nothing ever bound one (WebKit shipped its own inspector). A dedicated inspector window now hosts that page: one shared window that re-points to the current page on each invocation, creates its DevTools page on the inspected page's own profile (private windows included), keeps JavaScript enabled for itself even under the Safest security tier, and releases the binding when it closes or when the inspected page goes away.

Settings navigation (UIP03):
    The Preferences dialog now uses Vivaldi-style vertical navigation: a fixed-width sidebar on the left lists the nine sections (with icons) and drives a stacked page area, replacing the horizontal tab row. Every page's contents are unchanged, sections are reachable by mouse and keyboard, and the remembered "last open page" preference carries over with the same ordering.

Tools menu (MENU01):
    The Tools menu drops two redundant entries: "Web Search" (searching is done from the omnibox address bar — or the dedicated search box if you enabled it) and "Configure Search Engines..." (engine management lives on the Settings > Search page). The Ctrl+K shortcut is unaffected: it still focuses the search box when the box is shown, or the address bar otherwise.
    The search box's own engine menu now opens the Settings > Search page directly instead of a separate dialog; the standalone engine manager dialog remains reachable through that page's "Manage..." button.

Search settings layout (SRCH04):
    The Search preferences page was reworked to the Vivaldi-style layout: three engine selectors up front — the default engine, a new "Private window engine" (private and Tor windows search through a different engine when configured, falling back to the default), and an "Image search engine" driving the new "Search Image with <engine>" image context-menu entry. Only engines advertising an image-search endpoint are listed; the bundled DuckDuckGo, Google and Yahoo! descriptors carry one.
    The search field display is now an explicit choice — "Show as a Text Field" or "Show as a Button": button mode collapses the box to its engine button whose menu leads with a "Search..." prompt, and it shares the same settings key as the previous show/hide option, so existing opt-ins carry over. The page also gains "Show Search Engine Nickname in Drop-Down Menu", "Always Search in New Tab", "Show Search Engine Icon", "Keep Last Selected Search Engine", "Keep Typed Text After Searching" and "Page Selection Search in Background" (context-menu "Search with" results can open unfocused). The search field's own engine pick is honored per widget — in a private window it sets the private engine — and reverts to the default after each search unless "keep last selected" is on.
    Search suggestions gained per-context toggles — "In Address Field", "In Search Field" and "Only When Using Search Engine Nickname" — layered on top of the per-engine opt-in; no keystroke leaves the address bar or search field unless the engine itself is opted in. Two screenshot items were deliberately skipped: there is no speed dial, and extension control of search awaits a security review.
    Engine upgrades reach existing profiles now: startup merges bundled descriptors that are missing from the saved engine list (a removal blocklist keeps user-deleted bundled engines deleted), and "Restore Defaults" in the engine manager truly refreshes same-named bundled descriptors — previously a stale saved copy shadowed the bundled file forever, so this is how the new image-search endpoints arrive on existing installs.

Search engine editor (SRCH05):
    The Search settings page now edits engines inline instead of opening a separate manager dialog: an engine list with icons, nickname column and [IMAGE]/[PRIVATE] badges sits beside a live editor for the name, nickname(s), search/suggest/image-search URLs and POST parameters, plus "Set as Default Search" and "Set as Private Search" checkboxes that write straight through to the selectors above. Add, Remove, Up/Down reordering and Restore Defaults work on the page itself; engine order is now a real preference (persisted and honored by every engine dropdown and menu, not just the editor). Renaming an engine re-keys its default/private/image/field assignments, suggestion opt-ins and saved descriptor file rather than orphaning them.
    Edits validate before they apply — names must be non-empty and unique, nicknames must not collide with another engine's, URLs need a %s or {searchTerms} slot (or POST parameters carrying it) — and bad input reverts with an explanation instead of being silently kept. The standalone engine manager dialog still exists as the fallback when no main window is around to host the settings page.

Search shortcut nicknames (SRCH06):
    The Search preferences page gained a "Shortcuts Nickname" group: Bookmarks, History and Tabs each get a checkbox and an editable nickname (defaulting to @bookmarks, @history and @tabs). Typing a nickname followed by a space at the start of the address bar scopes the completion dropdown to that source — @bookmarks lists matching bookmarks, @history lists only history hits, and @tabs lists the window's open tabs; picking a tab result switches to it instead of reloading. Search-engine keywords still take precedence when a nickname collides, and an unchecked row's token is treated as ordinary search text.
    A "Reset Search Settings to Default" button at the bottom of the page restores every preference the page owns — engine picks, suggestion switches, shortcut nicknames and display options — after a confirmation prompt, leaving all other settings pages untouched.

Status bar widgets (UIP04):
    The status bar gained two permanent right-side widgets, Vivaldi-style: a page-load indicator and a zoom control. While a page loads, the indicator shows a slim progress bar with a running elapsed timer; on completion it reports "Loaded in N s" for a few seconds before clearing itself — it lives outside the temporary-message area so link-hover text never clobbers it. The zoom control reads "- 100% +": the buttons step the current tab through the standard zoom ladder, the percent itself resets to 100%, and the display follows menu, keyboard and Ctrl+wheel zoom as well as each tab's own level on every tab switch. Both honor the existing status-bar show/hide preference.

AdBlock toolbar button (ADB05):
    A content-blocker button now sits at the right end of the address bar, uBO-style. Its tinted shield icon carries a small numeric badge counting the requests blocked on the current page, and greys out while content blocking is disabled in Settings or the site is whitelisted. Clicking it opens a compact menu with the per-page blocked count, a "Content blocking" enable toggle and an "Ad Block Settings..." entry that opens the existing configuration dialog; Tools > Ad Block is unchanged. The counts come from a new session tally inside the profile request interceptor, keyed by the page's first-party host: plain blocks and redirects to bundled stub resources count, $removeparam query rewrites do not.

CSP and sandbox conformance audit (SEC14):
    The browseraudit.com CSP-category warnings turned out to be stale test expectations rather than browser defects, verified by a new autotests/csp regression suite (15 cases) that replays the same shapes against in-process HTTP/HTTPS/WebSocket endpoints: the bundled Chromium 140 engine correctly allows a same-origin wss:// connection under default-src/connect-src 'self' (the CSP3 matching rule — 'self' on an https origin covers its wss variant — that the 2015-era suite predates), while still blocking cross-host, cross-port and *-src 'none' attempts before they reach the network. Sandboxed iframes behave per spec too: allow-same-origin children can read and write same-site cookies, opaque-origin children cannot, and Arora's third-party cookie filter does not interfere. No application code strips or rewrites CSP headers — profile request interceptors are request-side only and never see response headers. Findings and per-test evidence are documented in .devin/SEC14-report.md.

browseraudit.com full-suite baseline (SEC15):
    Fixed a real subframe-loading regression found by the audit: Chromium's built-in error pages are enabled again, so a failed iframe/frame load now commits an error document and fires its load event like other browsers — pages that wait on a subframe's load event (including failed loads) no longer hang. The custom Arora error page for failed main-frame loads is unchanged.
    A new headless harness (`arora --browseraudit-smoke`, plus a bare-engine `--browseraudit-bare` control and an ARORA_AUDIT_WIRE bisection switch) runs the complete 431-test browseraudit.com suite and captures per-test outcomes to JSON. Baseline on Chromium 140: 383 pass / 48 warning / 0 critical / 0 skip on the real profile, every warning classified — full report with per-category table in .devin/SEC15-browseraudit.md (raw captures in .devin/sec15/). Highlights: no critical failures anywhere; the app adds no security gaps over the bare engine; most warnings are 2015-era test expectations (Content-Length is now a CORS-safelisted header, CSP3 'self' covers wss:, the site's own HTTPS redirect breaks its plain-http HSTS probes, and 300 ms observation windows race modern Chromium's deferred worker/manifest/report scheduling). One real engine gap: QtWebEngine never fetches <link rel="manifest"> documents, so manifest-src allow-tests warn.

XS-Leak audit baseline classified (XSLEAK02):
    The xsinator.com cross-site leak battery was baseline-measured and every vector dispositioned in .devin/XSLEAK01-report.md. All 18 leaking vectors trace to engine-side surfaces Chromium's embedder API cannot reach (subresource load/error oracles, frame counting, resource timing, redirect and URL-length probes, CORP/CORB, download detection) — the report names the specific missing API for each. Arora's own code is not implicated: the application network manager and its disk cache are not in the page-load path, the download accept path is not observable to page script, and the custom scheme handlers already refuse remote initiators. The app's defaults do explain two of the three timed-out vectors — the popup blocker dead-ends feature-string window.open calls used by test orchestration, and the privacy ping blocker stops CSP report delivery — which the follow-up fix pass will attribute before any engine write-off.

WebChannel console noise eliminated (CHAN01):
    The recurring "Uncaught TypeError: channel.execCallbacks[message.id] is not a function" errors on every launch and page load are gone. The cause was transport contention: qt.webChannelTransport exposes a single onmessage slot per frame and each new QWebChannel client replaces it, so the injected autofill client and page-side clients (like the start page's own) stole each other's in-flight responses. A new page bootstrap (armed at document creation) now demultiplexes that slot across clients — responses are routed to whichever client's callback table owns their id — and Arora's own features share one lazy channel client instead of each opening its own. The start page's engine switcher, the consent-gated AddSearchProvider hook and the autofill save prompt all keep working alongside page-created channel clients.

User agent (UA03):
    The default user agent now presents a newer Chrome milestone ("Chrome/155" instead of the bundled Chromium's real "Chrome/140"), because sites that version-sniff the UA nag "browser out of date" as soon as the engine's milestone lags Chrome stable. This is presentation only — the engine underneath is still the bundled Chromium, and no feature gates change; if a site breaks anyway, the User Agent menu's override or a preset is the escape hatch. The presented number lives in one place (BrowserProfile::presentedChromeMajor) for easy bumping.
    The client hints tell the same story instead of leaking the real version: both the "Chromium" and "Google Chrome" brands now carry the presented milestone over the real build's tail ("155.0.<build>.<patch>" — the same reduced-UA/full-version shape real Chrome sends), so the UA string, navigator.userAgentData and the Sec-CH-UA headers agree instead of fingerprinting a mismatch. Platform tokens stay accurate — only the Chrome version number is presented.

TLS cipher suites (TLS01):
    Arora no longer offers weak cipher suites in the TLS ClientHello: the RSA key-exchange suites (TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256, TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384, TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA, TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA — none provide forward secrecy) and the two ECDHE CBC-SHA suites are dropped via Chromium's --cipher-suite-blacklist switch, leaving TLS 1.3 and the ECDHE+AEAD set. This only narrows what is advertised — servers that negotiate strong suites are unaffected, but a site that supports ONLY the removed suites will not load. The Privacy preference "Offer only strong TLS cipher suites" (on by default) is the escape hatch for such ancient sites, and a --cipher-suite-blacklist already present in QTWEBENGINE_CHROMIUM_FLAGS wins over the built-in list. The flag latches when the engine starts, so toggling the preference needs a restart.

Location bar layout (UIP05):
    The site-privacy shield moved from the trailing button cluster to the left end of the address bar, Vivaldi-style — shield first, then the site icon, then the address text — so the privacy/identity controls sit together in the leading zone. The panel still opens anchored under the icon, and the content-blocker button keeps its spot at the right end.

WebRTC and DNS leak verification (LEAK01):
    Verified empirically that the WebRTC IP-handling hardening actually engages: a new headless probe (`arora --webrtc-smoke`) loads a page that creates a real RTCPeerConnection and enumerates its ICE candidates — with "Prevent WebRTC from exposing IP addresses" on (the default), Chromium's disable_non_proxied_udp policy leaves no UDP transport at all and zero candidates are emitted, not even mDNS-obfuscated ones. The unprotected control run (`arora --webrtc-off-smoke`) still produces candidates, proving the probe exercises real WebRTC machinery — the earlier suspicion that the engine flag latched too late was unfounded, it lands before the browser starts. The engine flag is process-global, so normal, private and Tor profiles are all covered; the matching per-profile WebRTCPublicInterfacesOnly attribute stays as a second layer.
    DNS story documented honestly in the README: with secure DNS off or in automatic-upgrade mode the browser resolves through the system resolver and lookups are visible to the local network — only a strict custom DoH endpoint (Privacy settings) or the Tor window (whose SOCKS5 route tunnels DNS too) hides them, and a system VPN or DoH resolver is the whole-OS answer.

ipduh.com anonymity-check audit (ANON01):
    A new headless harness (`arora --anon-smoke`) drives the live ipduh.com/privacy-test page on the real browsing profile and captures every section it reports — IP/ASN/geolocation, resolver identification, request-header anomaly scan, JS system-info probes, fonts, storage/cookie readbacks — plus direct navigator/timezone/WebRTC-ICE measurements, to a JSON file for the committed audit report (.devin/ANON01-report.md).
    The audit confirms the existing hardening holds on a live check site: the wire UA and client hints are a consistent Chrome/155 story, the request headers carry no Via/X-Forwarded-For-style anomalies, and an in-page RTCPeerConnection ICE gather emits zero candidates with WebRTC IP protection on. Enabling "Report a UTC timezone" flips the site's timezone read to UTC end-to-end. The remaining flags are classified rather than fixed: timezone/language/DNS are covered by existing opt-in privacy toggles, while font enumeration, hardwareConcurrency/deviceMemory and screen geometry are engine-side surface QtWebEngine exposes no API for — documented in the report instead of faked.

Pop-up blocking (POPUP01):
    "Block Popup Windows" is now a real pop-up blocker instead of only the engine's gesture check — the preference used to map straight to QWebEngineSettings::JavascriptCanOpenWindows, which drops gesture-less window.open calls but let every click-triggered pop-up (the common case, e.g. kephyr.com's pop-up killer test) open a normal window. The decision now lives in WebPage::createWindow keyed on the window shape Chromium reports: WebBrowserWindow and WebDialog requests (window.open with a feature string) dead-end while the blocker is on, while WebBrowserTab and WebBrowserBackgroundTab requests keep following the "open target-blank links in" preference — tab links are unaffected. The JavascriptCanOpenWindows binding stays as an upstream layer so gesture-less plain window.open calls cannot slip through tab-shaped as pop-unders.
    A refused pop-up keeps a live window handle through a dead-end probe page that rejects all navigation but reports the first http(s)/ftp destination back, so it can be offered for "Open once". A new indicator appears at the right end of the address bar next to the content-blocker button while the current page has blocked pop-ups: a count-badged icon whose menu lists the tally, an "Open" entry per captured destination (opened in a new tab), "Always allow pop-ups on <host>" and a "Pop-up Settings..." shortcut. The tally is per-document — it survives fragment/same-document navigations and resets on real ones.
    Exceptions key on the site showing the pop-up (a rule for example.com covers its subdomains), persist under the popupExceptions settings group, and are managed from three places: the indicator menu, the site panel's new "Allow pop-ups on this site" toggle, and the Privacy page's remembered-permissions list where they appear as removable "Pop-ups / Allowed" rows. Off-the-record pages write session-only exceptions — nothing private is persisted. The preference keeps its name and settings key so existing profiles carry over.

Referer hardening (REF01):
    The old on/off "trim Referer" toggle is now a level selector on the Privacy page — Chromium default / Trimmed / Strict / Never — stored under privacy/refererPolicy with the old boolean migrating automatically. Trimmed (the default) sends cross-site requests the destination's own origin and same-site requests only the origin, never the full path or query; Strict sends nothing to other sites; Never withholds the header everywhere. A document-start script pins Referrer-Policy inside each page so subresources, redirects, meta-refreshes and form posts — paths the request interceptor cannot rewrite — follow the same level; stricter site-declared policies still win. The Tor window enforces at least Trimmed regardless of the normal-profile setting.
    `./arora --referer-smoke` verifies the darklaunch matrix end to end on loopback: images, scripts, iframes and fetch/XHR subresources, direct and noreferrer navigation, cross-site and same-site redirect chains, meta refresh, form POST, and page- or header-declared no-referrer policies; both smokes now suspend the user's live adblock subscriptions while running so real filters cannot eat the fixtures. Known residual: the engine recomputes the referer on cross-site redirect responses itself, so an exit-redirect reveals the source's origin (never the path) — full findings in .devin/REF01-report.md.

Beacon / ping / CSP-report blocking (PING01):
    A new "Block tracking pings and beacons" preference on the Privacy page (privacy/blockPings, default on) drops the invisible upload channels pages use for click and navigation tracking: navigator.sendBeacon beacons, <a ping> hyperlink audits and Content-Security-Policy violation reports all ride Chromium's ping/report request channel and are failed in the request interceptor. Hyperlink auditing is also switched off in the profile settings so <a ping> requests never initiate at all, and the Tor window applies the same drop — a telemetry POST is the last thing that should slip through there.
    CSP enforcement is unaffected: a page's policy still blocks and sandboxes content exactly as declared — only the violation report upload is suppressed (sites lose the telemetry, users lose the tracking channel — the standard hardening tradeoff).
    `./arora --ping-smoke` proves it end to end on a loopback fixture: with the toggle on, the click's real navigation lands but the beacon, a-ping and CSP uploads never reach the server; with the toggle off all three arrive, so the clean pass is not vacuous. Verified over WAN against a webhook endpoint — zero requests escaped with blocking on.

HTTPS-Only mode (SAFE01):
    A new "Warn before loading a site over plain HTTP" preference on the Privacy page (privacy/httpsOnly, default on) closes the gap HTTPS-First left open: the upgrade used to decide silently, and an http page whose https attempt failed (or every http link with the upgrade off) loaded without any warning. Now any main-frame navigation still resolving to http: after the upgrade pass is refused — in acceptNavigationRequest for the navigations it sees, and in the request interceptor for every redirect hop — and swapped for a warning page explaining that the site only answers over an unencrypted connection.
    The warning page reuses the certificate-error interstitial machinery: it is served from a new internal arora-http-warning: scheme carrying a random nonce, so web content cannot forge its action links. "Proceed anyway" records a session-scoped exception for the host and re-loads it; "Always allow HTTP on this site" persists a per-host exception (off-the-record profiles only ever get the session-scoped choice); "Back to safety" returns to the previous page or the start page. Exceptions also appear as an "Always allow insecure HTTP on this site" toggle in the site panel. Loopback, LAN/private addresses and .onion names are exempt, and the Tor window never warns. The certificate-error and HTTPS-Only pages now share one nonce-keyed interstitial registry (publishInterstitialPage) served by one scheme handler class.
    `./arora --httpsonly-smoke` verifies it against a recording loopback proxy: upgrade ordering (https tried first, warn only after the downgrade), the warning commit, proceed re-loading the target, a mid-chain redirect to http still warning, https traffic untouched, loopback exempt, and the toggle-off path loading plain http.

Insecure form-submission warning (SAFE02):
    Submitting a form to a public http: destination now asks first: a warning dialog explains that anyone on the network path could read or change the data — including passwords — with "Submit Anyway" and Cancel choices (Cancel aborts the submission). Loopback, LAN/private addresses, .local and .onion targets stay silent, as does everything in a Tor window, and https forms never prompt.
    The confirmation is remembered per destination host for the page that asked, so one "Submit Anyway" does not nag again on the next post to the same host but a different destination still asks. HTTPS-Only mode keeps precedence — a non-excepted http navigation shows the SAFE01 warning page instead, never both dialogs — and a host HTTPS-First can still upgrade is exempt until its upgrade fails, at which point the plaintext fallback warns again.

Page-load hang with adblock enabled (STALL01):
    Fixed a hang that stalled every page load once real filter lists were installed. uBO rules carry constraint options Arora's parser did not understand (from=, to=, method=, ip=, cname, and similar), and silently dropping a constraint widened the rule: *$script,redirect-rule=noopjs,from=<site>,to=<host> degraded into a redirect-every-script filter, and its arora-resource:/noop.js target was then fed back through the same interceptor where it matched the rule again, churning the redirect chain while similarly globalized image rules blocked every picture. Two fixes: only http/https/ws/wss requests now reach the filter lists (the adblock, privacy and Tor interceptors early-return every internal or non-web scheme — devtools:, chrome:, qrc:, arora-*:, abp:, data: and friends pass through untouched), and any option that would change matching semantics but cannot be honored now makes the rule inert instead of broadening it. Common uBO aliases are understood properly: 3p/1p party spellings, frame/doc/beacon type aliases, ehide/ghide and queryprune.
    `.devin/stall01-repro.sh` replays the failure deterministically — a seeded list containing both the original widened rules and a deliberately global *$script redirect — asserting 3 real pages x 10 iterations each finish under 10 s (30/30 pass; the unfixed code manages 0/3). New autotest coverage pins the scheme gate and proves a self-matching redirect rule terminates instead of looping.

Address-bar anti-phishing surface (SAFE03):
    The address bar now emphasizes the registrable domain in a displayed url: while the bar is not being edited, the scheme, userinfo, subdomains, port and path render dimmed and the effective TLD+1 stays full-strength — the paypal.com.evil.tld trick can no longer hide the real domain inside a long subdomain run. Qt 6 dropped QUrl::topLevelDomain and its public-suffix table, so multi-label registry boundaries (co.uk, com.au and friends) ride the cookie jar's two-level ccTLD list and everything else assumes a one-label suffix; hosts with too few labels, IP literals and localhost emphasize whole. The emphasis is repaint-only — the underlying text is unchanged and editing, selecting or focusing the bar shows it plainly.
    Internationalized host names keep showing their punycode (xn--) form in the bar — a homograph like the Cyrillic "рayрal" can never masquerade as the ascii spelling — and hovering the bar offers a tooltip with the Unicode form for readability.

Scrollable Preferences pages (UIP06):
    Fixed the Preferences dialog opening taller than the screen with the OK/Cancel buttons unreachable — on small screens, scaled displays or large fonts the tallest settings page used to push the whole button row out of view, making it impossible to apply any setting. Every settings page now lives in its own scroll area, so an over-tall page scrolls instead of growing the window, and the dialog itself can never open larger than ~90% of the available screen. Page contents, the section sidebar, the page order and the remembered "last open page" preference are unchanged.

Prefetch and remote-font blocking (SAFE04):
    Two new request-layer blocks on the Privacy page: "Block prefetch requests" (privacy/blockPrefetch, default on) drops <link rel=prefetch> and speculation-rules prefetch loads — pages fetched only because a link pointed at them, leaking connections to sites the user never visited — and "Block remote web fonts" (privacy/blockRemoteFonts, default off — opt-in, since icon fonts break) drops downloadable font files, a fingerprinting and tracking channel, while local and system fonts keep working. The Tor window honors both toggles.
    Speculation-rules prefetch navigations reach the request interceptor disguised as ordinary page loads, but Chromium marks every prefetch flavor with a "Purpose"/"Sec-Purpose: prefetch" request header — both are matched, not just the prefetch resource type. `./arora --resourceblock-smoke` proves it end to end on a loopback fixture: armed toggles never let a font or prefetch request reach the wire, and the controls confirm the fixture can deliver them.

Isolated container profiles (CONT01):
    Firefox-style containers begin with the profile core: a ContainerManager keeps a persistent registry of named containers (id, name, color) and lazily creates one dedicated WebEngine profile per container under a "containers/<id>" storage tree — giving each container its own cookies, cache, local storage and service workers. Every container profile is prepared through the same path as the normal profile, so the privacy/adblock interceptor, custom scheme handlers, cookie jar, download manager and all WebEngine settings apply identically inside containers.
    Settings changes, user-agent/client-hints updates, clear-private-data and clear-on-exit now reach every materialized container profile, and deleting a container removes its registry entry plus its on-disk storage. Tor mode hands out no container profile at all — containers are persistent state and can never live on the Tor session.

Per-tab containers (CONT02):
    Tabs can now live inside a container: a new tab inherits its opener's container, File → New Container Tab opens one directly in any container (or "No Container"), and the tab context menu's "Reopen in Container" submenu swaps a tab into a different container in place, keeping its URL. Container tabs wear a colored strip plus the container's name as a chip in the tab bar, so the boundary stays visible at a glance; renaming, recoloring or deleting a container refreshes the indicators live.
    The session file records each tab's container (older sessions restore into the default container, and a deleted container's tabs fall back to it rather than being dropped), and recently-closed tabs reopen in the container they came from. Private windows and Tor windows are excluded — persistent containers can never hold off-the-record browsing. Cookie isolation between containers is verified by an autotest that loads real pages on two container profiles and a default profile and confirms each cookie jar sees only its own container's cookie.

Cross-site WebSocket blocking (XSLEAK03):
    A new opt-in "Block third-party WebSockets" toggle on the Privacy preferences page (privacy/blockThirdPartyWebSockets, default off) refuses ws:/wss: upgrade requests whose target host is not same-site with the page — a connect/refuse difference is one of the classic XS-Leak oracles. It is off by default because legitimate real-time features (chat widgets, feeds) can deliberately use cross-site sockets; same-site sockets are always allowed. The Tor window honors the same setting.
    The XS-Leak audit also verified end to end that the existing "block third-party cookies" policy really engages: a real cross-site iframe's Set-Cookie is refused by the cookie filter and never sent back, and two earlier xsinator timeouts were attributed to deliberate defaults — the CSP-report block (privacy/blockPings) drops the report upload a CSP test waits for, and the popup blocker's dead-end probe page stalls window.open-driven tests. Remaining xsinator vectors are engine-side and documented in .devin/XSLEAK01-report.md with the exact missing QtWebEngine hooks.

Container management UI (CONT03):
    Settings gains a "Containers" page in the sidebar: it lists every registered container with its name and accent color, and offers New Container / Edit / Delete. New and Edit share a small editor with a name field and the fixed eight-color palette; a container with an empty name cannot be accepted. Delete warns that all site data in the container — cookies, logins, cache and site storage — is permanently removed, names how many open tabs will be closed, and closes them before the container's profile directory is wiped. An empty state explains the feature when no containers exist, and the page stays live: containers created, renamed or deleted elsewhere refresh the list as it happens. The "Manage Containers" menu entries now jump straight to the page.

Site-to-container rules (CONT04):
    Sites can now be pinned to a container, Firefox-style: "Always Open Site in Container" in the tab context menu (a checkbox on container tabs, a container picker on default tabs) records a persistent host-to-container rule, and every later main-frame navigation to that host — or any of its subdomains — is refused on the wrong profile and reopened in a fresh tab bound to the ruled container. Redirect hops re-enter the same check, so a chain crossing between ruled hosts keeps landing each leg in its own container; a rate bound stops a cyclic cross-container chain from spawning tabs forever.
    Rules normalize what they store (lowercase, trailing dot dropped, one "www." folded to the apex so a rule covers both spellings — unless the apex is a public suffix), are listed and removable per container on the Settings Containers page, and die with their container. Private and Tor windows never divert, non-HTTP(S) addresses are exempt, and a form submission that diverts is re-issued as a plain load — a POST body can never be carried into another container's storage.

XS-Leak verification pass (XSLEAK04):
    The full xsinator.com battery was re-run against the shipping profile after the mitigation work: every still-leaking vector reproduces identically with the pop-up and ping blocks disabled, confirming each is an engine-internal surface (subresource load/error oracles, frame counting, resource timing, redirect and URL-length probes, CORP/CORB, download detection) that no QtWebEngine embedder API can reach — the report names the missing hook per vector. Two baseline leaks no longer reproduce at all: the WebSocket pool oracle is mitigated by Chromium's randomized pool cap (the suite reports it directly), and the cross-origin contentDocument probe now returns clean; all three baseline timeouts resolved or were attributed to the deliberate pop-up/ping defaults. The audit closes with zero leaks attributable to application code.

Container isolation audit (CONT05):
    The container boundary is now proven end to end on a loopback fixture: cookies, localStorage, IndexedDB, service-worker registrations, the http cache and the http-auth credential cache planted on one container's profile are verified absent from a second container and from the default profile, and the composite privacy/adblock interceptor plus profile settings are asserted on every materialized container profile. Private windows now refuse containers at the profile layer too — ContainerManager::profileFor returns nothing while private browsing is on (Tor was already refused), so a persistent profile can never back an off-the-record window.
    Two hygiene gaps the audit found are closed: deleting a container that was never opened in the current session also removes the QtWebEngine-derived cache and default-storage directories (previously only the storage root went away), and Clear Private Data / clear-on-exit now reach containers whose profile was never materialized — their on-disk cookies, site data, cache and visited links are wiped directly instead of silently surviving the clear. The isolation boundary, including the deliberately global surfaces (history, downloads, autofill/passwords) and the shared network-service bound (DNS, socket pools, TLS resumption are process-level in Chromium), is documented in the README's Containers section.

Reader mode (READ01):
    The View menu gains a checkable "Reader Mode" action (Ctrl+Alt+R) and the location bar shows a page icon whenever a page looks article-like; both toggle a clutter-free reader overlay. Article extraction runs client-side with the bundled Mozilla Readability.js (Apache-2.0) — injected only on activation — and renders into a Shadow DOM overlay with serif typography, a light/dark theme following the application palette, persistent font-size controls and an "Exit reader view" button, so exiting restores the original page, scroll position and form state without a reload. Pages that do not look like articles are refused with a status-bar message, the overlay works in private windows, and it still opens on pages whose scripts are blocked — the injection is app-side and lifts JavascriptEnabled only for the injection window.

Sleeping tabs (SLEEP01):
    Tabs can now be suspended to reclaim memory without losing their place on the strip. An opt-in "Suspend tabs after N minutes idle" setting (Settings > Tabs, off by default) sweeps background tabs and discards their web content through the engine's page-lifecycle API — the slot keeps its title and favicon, the title dims and a "zZ" badge marks it, and clicking the tab wakes it with a reload that restores the captured scroll position. The tab context menu also offers "Sleep Tab" / "Wake Tab" for manual control; a manual sleep overrides the media exemptions (a valid way to silence a tab) while the automatic sweep skips the current tab, recently-audible pages, tabs with an in-flight download, tabs still loading, and tabs holding unsaved form input — a refused suspend is explained on the status bar. Preserved across a sleep: the tab slot, title, favicon, history entries and scroll position; transient form content is not, which is why dirty forms are never suspended.

Command palette (CMD01):
    A Ctrl+Shift+P command palette (also in the Tools menu) fuzzy-searches every browser action, open tab, bookmark title and settings section from one keyboard-first overlay: arrows navigate, Enter executes, Esc dismisses, and recently run entries float to the top. Typing "clear history" reaches Clear Private Data, "privacy" jumps straight to that settings page, and tab titles switch tabs; bookmark entries load the page in the current tab.

Picture-in-Picture (PIP01):
    Videos can now pop out into a small frameless, always-on-top player window: right-click a video and choose "Picture-in-Picture", use the checkable View → "Picture-in-Picture" action for the largest playing video, or click a site's own PiP button — requestPictureInPicture() is shimmed to route through the browser with the same user-gesture requirement Chromium applies, and document.exitPictureInPicture() closes the window again. The window replays the stream on the page's own profile (logins and local files keep working), shows a drag handle plus "return to tab" and close controls, and hands the playback position back to the page element — which sits behind an in-page placeholder while popped out. Navigating away closes the window; sources that cannot be re-attached (DRM or some blob/MSE streams) restore the original player with an explanatory message instead of failing silently. Qt WebEngine ships no PiP delegate — the PiP window manager lives in Chrome's browser layer outside the content API — so this is an application-side implementation rather than the engine's native PiP.

Tab groups (TABGRP01):
    Tabs can be organized into named, color-coded groups, Vivaldi/Chrome-style. Right-click a tab to create a group ("New Group"), add it to an existing one, rename it, pick a color from the eight-color palette, or ungroup; dragging a tab onto a group member joins that group, and dragging a grouped tab away from its neighbors ungroups it. A group's tabs stay contiguous on the strip and a colored header chip bearing the group's name leads the run; "Collapse Group" folds the group down to just that chip (its tabs stay loaded invisibly) and clicking the chip expands it again. New tabs opened from a grouped tab inherit the group, closing a collapsed chip expands rather than strands its hidden members, and groups survive session save/restore (sessions written by older versions still restore, and older builds ignore the new group data).

Sidebar panel (SIDE01):
    An optional Vivaldi-style sidebar can now dock to the left or right edge of the window — off by default, so the interface is unchanged unless it is switched on. Toggle it from View → "Sidebar", the F4 key, the toolbar button, or Settings → General → "Show sidebar"; the same dialog also chooses whether it docks left or right. The panel hosts four tabs reusing the existing data: Bookmarks (the full tree with search filter, folders open in place, links load in the current tab), History (the shared history tree with filter), Downloads (the live download list — double-click opens finished files, a button opens the download manager), and Notes (a plain-text pad saved automatically). Visibility, dock side, the active tab and notes are all remembered across sessions; until the panel is first shown it is never constructed, so the default experience carries zero cost.

Link sharing (POL01):
    Right-click menus gain "Copy Clean Link", which copies the link — or the page address when clicked on plain page content — with tracking parameters stripped: utm_*, fbclid, gclid, msclkid, Mailchimp/HubSpot/Marketo identifiers and ~50 more names from a curated blocklist (extendable without a rebuild via the urlcleaner/extraParams settings key). Other query parameters, their order, and the #fragment are preserved; only http(s) URLs are touched.
    Every context menu also offers "Show QR Code for This Page", popping a small dialog that renders the current page address as a scannable QR code (vendored MIT-licensed qrcodegen encoder) with the URL shown selectable underneath.

Tab strip polish (POL02):
    Ctrl+Shift+A (or Window → "Search Tabs...") opens a tab search: the command palette restricted to the open-tabs list across every window, fuzzy-filtered by title — Enter switches straight to the match. The full palette is unchanged; tabs-only is a per-open mode, not a sticky filter.
    Resting the pointer on a tab for half a second now pops a hover card showing the page's title, address and a live thumbnail snapshot; sleeping tabs and pages with no captured frame fall back to the favicon + title card, flagged "Sleeping". The card is a passive tooltip-style popup — it never takes focus and drops the moment the pointer leaves the strip, a button is pressed, the strip scrolls, or the window loses activation.

HTTPS-First downgrade fix (SAFE07):
    Fixed the HTTPS-First upgrade producing a bogus "This site does not support HTTPS" warning on healthy sites — a typed address like github.com could end up warned instead of silently upgraded. Any main-frame load failure used to record the host as "no https" for the whole session: aborted navigations (redirect hops, pressing Stop), requests refused by the adblock or privacy veto, DNS hiccups and proxy errors all counted, and once recorded the next plain-http visit skipped the upgrade and showed the warning page. Worse, the downgrade set was shared process-wide, so a failure inside a private or Tor window could poison the host for normal windows too.
    Now only genuine TLS/connectivity evidence downgrades a host — refused, reset, closed or timed-out connections and handshake failures inside Chromium's connection error class; vetoes, aborts, interrupted redirects, resolver faults and proxy plumbing errors never mark. Marks are scoped per profile (normal, private and each container get their own), expire after about half an hour so a transient outage is re-probed rather than condemned for the session, and are dropped as soon as an https load to the host actually commits. The Tor window never records marks — every failure there is attributed to the SOCKS tunnel, which says nothing about the site's TLS support.

Page polish (POL03):
    The find bar now reports a live "n/m" match counter — the active match ordinal and total from the render process — and still says "Not Found" for zero hits; emptying the field clears the counter together with the page highlights.
    Settings → Appearance gains "Render pages in dark mode": Chromium's auto-dark mode inverts light pages site-by-site while the browser's own chrome keeps the desktop theme. Note the auto-inversion can mangle pages that are already dark or carry deliberate light branding — it is a convenience toggle, not a guaranteed readable result; turning it off restores the page instantly.
    Middle-click autoscroll is on by default on Linux/Windows and off on macOS (Settings → General → "Middle-click autoscroll"): pressing the middle button on a page enters Chromium's native grab-scroll reader. The toggle takes effect at next launch — the Blink feature flag is process-lifetime — and merges into any user-supplied --enable-blink-features list instead of clobbering it.

